  "worked_solution": "Suppose $I\u2286R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]\u2282I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-6,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,1].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{3},\u2026,$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-6,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-6,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=-4$ and $x=6,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{4}^{-}}g(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to -{4}^{+}}g(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{-}}g(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}g(x)=2\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-6,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,\u2026,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]\u2282[-6,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042449/assets/9e447c0672628ce8b304726f36885f18661bbba790ae34ca806aaae95a465bbb.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"",
